  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "If \\(c\\) is a real number and the negative of one of the solutions of \\(x^2-3x+c=0\\) is a solution of \\(x^2+3x-c=0,\\) then the solutions of \\(x^2-3x+c=0\\) are",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1,\\) \\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1,\\) \\(-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(0,\\) \\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(0,\\) \\(-3\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac32,\\) \\(\\frac32\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The negative-root condition is useful because substitution makes the two equations nearly identical. Let \\(r\\) be a solution of the first equation whose negative solves the second. Then\n\\[\nr^2-3r+c=0\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(-r)^2+3(-r)-c=r^2-3r-c=0.\n\\]\nSubtracting the second equality from the first cancels every term involving \\(r\\), leaving \\(2c=0\\). Thus \\(c=0\\).\n\nThe first equation is therefore\n\\[\nx^2-3x=x(x-3)=0,\n\\]\nwhose solutions are \\(0\\) and \\(3\\). With \\(c=0\\), the second equation is \\(x(x+3)=0\\), so its solutions are \\(0\\) and \\(-3\\). This verifies the required negative-root relationship. The answer is \\(0,3\\), choice C.",
